<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elam was an ancient region in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>. Its rulers were long-standing rivals of Mesopotamian states, clashing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, the Babylonian Empire and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>. It formed the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elam.jpg" alt="Elam" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur Wkotwica; derivative work by Morningstar1814 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elam_Map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then extended his campaigns into <b>Elam</b> to the east and up the Euphrates into Syria, reaching the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains towards the Mediterranean, while later legend carried his armies into Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashurbanipal's destruction of the Elamite capital Susa around 647 BCE, at the end of a devastating war that ended <b>Elam</b> as a major power, demonstrated the army's capacity for warfare of annihilation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…minor city into a recognised regional player, completing the walls, digging canals, winning modest victories and surviving in a world dominated by <b>Elam</b> to the east, the rival cities of Isin and Larsa to the south, and the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia under Shamshi-Adad I to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lowland capital of Susa, heavily influenced by Mesopotamian culture, coexisted with the highland core centred on Anshan, and this bifurcation meant that <b>Elam</b> could lose its lowlands to conquest while retaining an unconquered interior from which revival was always possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His inscriptions have him defeat 'mountainous <b>Elam</b>', Urua, Umma and Ur, destroy Kish, Akshak and Mari at a place called Antasur and kill Zuzu, king of Akshak.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Hammurabi had defeated <b>Elam</b>, Eshnunna and Larsa with the help of Mari’s troops, relations between the two kings broke down, perhaps over control of the Euphrates trade and the towns between their kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
